Pri-Elf-Mal-Cha, Genoless, Polyless, Survivor. My second ascended
priest ever, and quite possibly my most interesting ascension to date.
Started with spells Light and Healing, which allowed me to stride
through the first few levels without much difficulty. The common
problem with priests, finding a good weapon, was ever-present as
always, and it was only after the Mine's End (Wine Cellar, plenty of
holy waters), when Susanowo mercifully gifted me with Werebane - a
rather lousy weapon in itself, but it unlocked an advancement in
sabers, allowing me to use the Grayswandir I had liberated from an old
Archeologist bones file a while back, even if it was cursed and kept
blasting me. The same bones contained SDSM, which kinda annoyed me, as
I had just earlier wasted a wish for a GDSM.

Later on, an unfortunate polymorph accident tore my robe, but
fortunately I discovered a Cloak of Magic Resistance a while after,
which also allowed me to switch for the SDSM mentioned. Another
accident, involving tengu, cursed me with a teleportitis, which these
days had become more of an extreme annoyance than a real handicap, and
with which I agonized for half the dungeon, until I managed to
discover a ring of teleport control. Much later, I was finally able to
get rid of the problem permanently by having another tengu for
breakfast. No lesson learned: I still keep doing the tengu gamble, as
I have done ever since I learned the possibilities of them. The quest
was no trouble, Grayswandir (+7 by now) went through Nalzok like knife
through butter, and the Mitre was soon enchanced to +5 as well. Sadly,
even after spending all the wishes from the Castle, my AC was still
below average, with only -28.

But the real trouble came from the compelete and utter lack of rings
of conflict or levitation, and gold detection scrolls, despite turning
the entire dungeon and Gehennom upside-down for them. Even Perseus
gave no boots. Medusa's lake and Juiblex's swamp went with a couple of
earth scrolls and wands of cold. Vlad's tower gave not only life
saving (which I never needed), but also a magical breathing, which I
also took, since right now it looked like I'd have to nose dive at the
Water Plane.

There were more than one surprise encounters with mind flayers (master
or not), wiping away practically all the maps and most of the items.
Luckily, I had both the Identify and Magic Mapping at my spells, so
that wasn't that big of a problem after all. The ritual went with a
routine, and a scroll of taming, combined with the temple graveyard,
confusion, and a magic whistle, gave me plenty of meat-shields (bone-
shields, ectoplasm-shields, whatever) against the High Priest, who had
decided to ally himself with the Terrible Yendor Twins. After a great
and epic battle, I made my exit with all three dead, and the Amulet in
my pocket.

And that's when it got tough, thanks to the aforementioned lack of
gold detections. I had to painstakingly check every single step in
every single cave of the bloody Earth Plane, before advancing. On Air
Plane, a single blessed potion of levitation was enough to get me
through - despite the heavy resistance from the air elementals, which
all ganged up on me, thanks to the lack of Conflict. There was a point
when the buggers reduced me to a single hit point, but luckily I had a
good storage of blessed full healings.

Fire Plane was no trouble, apart from a couple of Archons and from
trying to really be careful around lava, lest I took a wrong step and
met a burning end. At the Water Plane I, indeed, had no choice but to
take a swim, which cost me every single scroll, book, and potion
(except for holy waters), but on the other hand proved a decent
protection against eels and krakens. There I spent a long while until
the Astral Plane, where I battled a cockatrice and wished that the
Riders weren't immune to stoning (everyone else took it for granite,
pun intended), and almost died in the hands of Pestilence. Third
altar's the charm, ascended, had a long laughter of joy and relief.
Final score: 4557562.

And to think this was only supposed to be a short sparring session to
prepare myself for the upcoming tournament... And I'm sure every
single one of you could tell a more interesting tale, but please spare
me from those.


